# Attorney General Edward W. Brooke, the Politics of Gradualism and Immediacy, and the Struggle for Civil Rights

**Authors:** Jordan O’Neal Alexander

## Abstract
In the second half of the twentieth century, Edward W. Brooke became one of the best known and most highly respected African American political leaders in the nation. He believed that he could achieve more for civil rights within the Republican Party than without. For Brooke, Republican Party membership was more than a tool to ensure reelection in Massachusetts, where African Americans comprised only 2 percent of the population. As a Republican, he shaped the civil rights legislative agenda. Brooke has fallen so far out of the dialogue of mid-twentieth-century civil rights leaders because he felt uncomfortable being compared to his contemporaries in the Black Freedom Struggle. Instead, he advocated a third path—one of bipartisanship, cooperation, and enlightened policymaking. This article pursues these answers through an analysis of the late senator’s commitment to government reform, bipartisanship, and protection of civil rights during his tenure as attorney general of Massachusetts from 1963 to 1966.

